translate_llm:
The North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO) countries are planning a comprehensive reorganization of their military forces stationed outside the United States since the Korean War in 1953. On August 16, President Bush stated that within the next decade, between 60,000 to 70,000 troops and approximately 100,000 family members and civilian workers currently stationed abroad would be redeployed back to the United States. However, this redeployment of personnel is not the sole objective of the American administration. Another goal is to strengthen alliances. The United States will continue to maintain a military contingent in Europe and focus its forces in Asia to address both current and future challenges, such as international terrorism. This plan for the reduction of troops along the borders of Europe, Asia, and the Persian Gulf aims to improve relations with host countries and local populations.

More than three years prior, the United States had already begun redistributing its military forces worldwide. Administration officials have consulted with members of Congress as well as NATO allies. President Bush emphasized that this reorganization of troops is a response to current challenges and prepares for future threats. "Over the next decade, we will deploy a more mobile and flexible force, which means having fewer troops stationed in one place. Some troops will be redeployed to new areas where they can quickly respond to unexpected threats. This plan will close some bases abroad that are no longer needed given the current security environment and for the protection of peace." "The world has changed dramatically," said President Bush, "and the presence of U.S. military forces must change with it, so that we can be more effective in demonstrating our strength and in safeguarding freedom and peace."

